    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Uniform-price auctions have been used in many applications, from selling Treasury bills to distributing licenses. Compared to discriminatory auctions, uniform-price auctions can promote fairness, simplicity, and participation. Some uniform-price auctions also protect small bidders by setting aside units for them. Despite its attractive features, the uniform-price auction can be susceptible to collusion. The key reason is that uniform-price auctions can have equilibria with much lower market prices than the winning bidders' values. As such, large bidders are incentivized to enter self-enforcing agreements that yield substantial profits for them and low revenues for the auctioneer. While flexible supply has been found to reduce low-price equilibria in uniform-price auctions, it can be impractical to implement in some settings. Thus, I propose a novel anti-collusion provision for the uniform-price auction that fixes total supply but deters collusion by making the units restricted to small bidders adjustable. By analyzing equilibria and relating them to bidders' utilities, I find that my provision eliminates many of the low-price equilibria in the uniform-price auction, including those that maximize colluders' total profit. In addition, I show that combining my anti-collusion provision with limits on permissible bids can further reduce low-price equilibria.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
